Q: Using C#, .net 8 WPF; bind an entire control instead of individual properties. How? A: <p>If you are using C# 14 or later, you can simplify your properties:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>protected void OnPropertyChanged([CallerMemberName]string propertyName = null)
{
    PropertyChanged?.Invoke(this, new PropertyChangedEventArgs(propertyName));
}

public string DebugMsg
{
    get;
    set { field = value; OnPropertyChanged(); }
}

public string OtherMsg
{
    get;
    set { field = value; OnPropertyChanged(); }
}
</code></pre>
<p>By using the <code>CallerMemberNameAttribute</code> you can simplify calling <code>OnPropertyChanged</code> by dropping the property name argument at the call sites. C# will automatically provide the property name.</p>
<p>You can also use semi-auto-properties where the <code>field</code> keyword represents the automatically created backing field. The getter is fully automatic and returns this very backing field. The setter assigns this field and contains the notify mechanism.</p>
<p>Since the property body does not contain any property or field name (apart from the generic <code>field</code> keyword), it is the same in all the properties. I.e., you can copy/paste the property body to other properties without any changes.</p>
<p>But as others have pointed out, your view-model must provide a property for every WPF property you want to bind.</p>
<p>However,if you want to assign the same BackColor to several controls dynamically, as an example, then you need to provide only one BackColor property for all those controls.</p>

Q: is there a way in PHP to check a typed property is not initialised AND not null? A: <p>Use <code>isset()</code></p>
<pre><code>public function getValue(): ?string {
  if (!isset($this-&gt;myProp) {
    $this-&gt;myProp = null;
  }

  return $this-&gt;myProp;
}
</code></pre>
